    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d81">

                <pb facs="0214" n="140" sameAs="frus:frus1941-43/pg140"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Hopkins Papers</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The President’s Special Assistant</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hopkins</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the President
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]<anchor n="1" xml:id="d81-f1"/></seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-12-29">December 29,
                        1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <pb facs="0215" n="141" xml:id="pg141"/>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">St. Pierre &amp;
                    Miquelon</hi></p>
                <p>The Prime Minister just phoned me at 6:45,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d81-f2"/> reading
                    me a cable from the Foreign Office to him, in which they indicate the most
                    strenuous objection to the ousting of the Free French from Miquelon.<anchor
                        n="3" xml:id="d81-f3"/></p>
                <p>They claim de Gaulle will not issue the orders to throw his commander out.</p>
                <p>The burden of the message was that the whole business would kick up an
                    unbelievable row, for which we could give no good public explanation. In spite
                    of the fact that de Gaulle acted in bad faith, the British don’t see how he can
                    be forced out and think that the use of force would be very bad.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>H. L. H.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d81-f1" n="1" xml:id="d81-f1-content">
                <p>The omitted paragraphs of this memorandum refer to the Joint Declaration of
                    Allied unity; see <hi rendition="#italic">post</hi>, <ref target="#pg373">p.
                        373</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d81-f2" n="2" xml:id="d81-f2-content">
                <p>Churchill was in Ottawa on December 29–30 and the morning of December 31,
                    returning to Washington late that evening.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d81-f3" n="3" xml:id="d81-f3-content">
                <p>See the editorial note on the Churchill-Hull conversation on December 28, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg131">p. 131</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
